<resources xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <style name="Theme.AshuDialer" parent="android:Theme.Material.Light.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <!--
          This was missing before, which is why the gesture/navigation bar
          area stayed the OS's own default (white on a light system theme)
          no matter what enableEdgeToEdge() or the runtime insets-controller
          calls in MainActivity did - those only ever control the nav bar's
          *icon* color, not its background, and a background that's already
          opaque from the static theme can't be made to show the app's
          content through it at runtime. Theme.AshuDialer.Call already had
          this line; the main app theme just never got the same treatment.
        -->
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:windowAnimationStyle">@null</item>
        <item name="android:windowDisablePreview">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar" tools:targetApi="23">true</item>
        <!--
          Same class of fix as Theme.AshuDialer.Call below: without this,
          MainActivity's window falls back to the platform's own near-black
          default background. MainActivity is what briefly becomes visible
          underneath as InCallActivity closes when a call ends, so leaving
          this unset here reintroduces the same black flash one layer down
          even with the call screen's own theme fixed.

          CORRECTION from an earlier version of this file: this previously
          used windowBackgroundFallback instead of windowBackground.
          windowBackgroundFallback is real, but it is NOT a "safe window
          background that shows briefly at launch/close" attribute - the
          platform only ever paints it during a live multi-window RESIZE, to
          fill area newly exposed by dragging the split-screen divider, if
          the app lags behind in drawing there. A normal single-window
          activity finish/launch - the exact black-flash bug this file is
          trying to fix - never goes through that code path at all, so the
          previous version of this fix was a complete no-op: it changed the
          XML but never actually painted during the bug. Plain
          windowBackground is the attribute the compositor paints for every
          window, including this transition gap, which is what actually
          stops the flash.
        -->
        <!--
          Static XML fallback ONLY - this paints before MainActivity.onCreate
          has run at all (e.g. the very first frame of process/window
          creation), which is earlier than any runtime call could reach. Once
          onCreate does run, it immediately overrides this with a
          ColorDrawable matching the actual active theme (see onCreate's
          window.setBackgroundDrawable call and ThemePreference's
          lastKnownThemeIdSync) - so this XML value is only ever visible for
          a moment even shorter than the gap it originally covered, and only
          on the very first cold-start frame. Left as Ocean's own color
          (call_window_background.xml / colors.xml) since Ocean is still the
          app's real default theme for a person who has never changed it -
          this is not the fix for a themed flash overall, just the earliest
          possible fallback before there's any runtime code running to know
          what theme is active.
        -->
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@drawable/call_window_background</item>
    </style>

    <style name="Theme.AshuDialer.Call" parent="android:Theme.Material.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:windowShowWallpaper">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowIsTranslucent">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowNoTitle">true</item>
        <item name="android:backgroundDimEnabled">false</item>
        <!--
          Previously this call window was translucent. That allowed the previous
          Activity/launcher surface to remain visible behind the Telecom call
          window during OEM composition hand-off, which is the source of the
          brief blue flash seen on dark themes. The in-call window is now fully
          opaque; its background is set before Compose renders and stays opaque
          for the entire Activity lifetime.

          Previously unset here, which meant the status bar fell back to
          whatever the platform's own Theme.Material.NoActionBar default is -
          varying by OEM/API level, and not guaranteed transparent or
          light/dark-consistent with anything this app draws. Setting it
          transparent here (matching Theme.AshuDialer's own status bar
          config) means the call screen's own background - which already
          extends edge-to-edge - shows through instead of a mismatched bar.
          The light/dark *icon* color on top of that transparent bar still
          needs to be set at runtime per theme, same as MainActivity does,
          since a static XML value can't follow the person's active palette -
          see InCallActivity.kt's WindowCompat call.
        -->
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:windowAnimationStyle">@null</item>
        <!-- Never let the platform show the application preview window between
             the telephony activity and its first Compose frame. On OEM builds
             that preview can use the application theme background (Ocean blue)
             even when the selected call theme is Black/Dark. -->
        <item name="android:windowDisablePreview">true</item>
        <!--
          Runtime-only now: InCallActivity.onCreate briefly sets a real
          ColorDrawable here via window.setBackgroundDrawable(), using the
          actual active theme's own background color, to cover the gap
          before this window's first Compose frame draws (see that comment
          for the full explanation - this XML attribute used to be a single
          hardcoded Ocean-blue drawable, which is why every OTHER theme,
          including Black, flashed that wrong color during the same gap).
          Once that first frame lands, a LaunchedEffect in setContent reverts
          this back to transparent so the window keeps behaving as a
          translucent window (Theme.AshuDialer.Call's windowIsTranslucent)
          for the rest of the call - needed for the fast reveal-the-dialer-
          underneath behavior on hang-up. Left transparent here in XML
          (rather than removed) purely as the correct static default for the
          rare case some other entry point creates this themed window
          without going through InCallActivity's onCreate at all.
        -->
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@drawable/call_window_background</item>
    </style>

    <style name="Theme.Transparent" parent="android:Theme.Material.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:windowIsTranslucent">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:windowNoTitle">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowAnimationStyle">@null</item>
        <item name="android:windowDisablePreview">true</item>
    </style>
</resources>
